Observers are designed to estimate the states of epidemic models, including the SIS, SIR, SIRa and S-I-R models, by leveraging only limited, easily accessible data such as non-linear measurements of ratio-dependent systems. These observers, which work well even when certain model parameters are uncertain, use a high-order extension approach and are further enhanced by a practical approach to recovering full state information when certain key parameters are known. Simulations, which test the designed observers in realistic scenarios, demonstrate their efficacy and highlight their potential applications in using observability to inform timely public health decision-making. Notably, these simulations illustrate the utility of these observers in cases where complete state information is not necessary for understanding the evolution of an epidemic. These results expand upon and improve previous work, which introduced observers for ratio-dependent systems but was limited to the SIS model and scenarios where parameters were fully known. 

